    "result_text": "All rejection paths now flow through the RFC7807 handler. \n\n## Summary\n\nI made all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, while keeping the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`RestRejectionException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying an `HttpStatus` plus a detail message, thrown by controllers when a business rule rejects a request.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for `RestRejectionException` that builds a `ProblemDetail` (via the existing `detailBuild` helper, which sets `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`) and returns it with `Content-Type: application/problem+json`. I also made every handler set that content type explicitly (previously the framework's default), so the bean-validation 400 path (`MethodArgumentNotValidException`) \u2014 which is what the missing-`city` acceptance test exercises \u2014 reliably emits `problem+json`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 replaced all twelve bare `return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST/CONFLICT/TOO_MANY_REQUESTS)` rejections (in `addOwner` and `updateOwner`) with `throw new RestRejectionException(...)` carrying a descriptive detail message. These previously returned an empty body; now they render a proper problem document. All rejection checks run before persistence, so throwing is safe.\n\nI confirmed no other controllers emit these statuses.\n\n**Test result:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` acceptance test, which asserts the exact `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status == 400`, passes).",
